        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "491eed8c-04d1-5ae2-86ed-a9b63cd82a3c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Heckscher State Parkway, Great River, Islip Terrace, Long Island, New York, Heckscher State Park, Town of Islip, Suffolk, New York City, Great South Bay, Atlantic Ocean, Fire Island\n- Systems: NY 27\n- Concepts: hamlet, census-designated place, suburban community\n\nFacts:\n- The Heckscher State Parkway, formerly known as the Heckscher Spur, is an 8.24-mile parkway on Long Island in New York, United States.\n- At Islip Terrace, the Heckscher State Parkway turns south, crosses NY 27, and ends at the toll barrier for Heckscher State Park in Great River.\n- Great River is a suburban hamlet and census-designated place in the Town of Islip in Suffolk, New York.\n- Great River is about 50 miles east of New York City, or about 55 miles by road.\n- Great River is on the South Shore of Long Island along the Great South Bay, and Fire Island protects it from the Atlantic Ocean.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
